{"name":"Earth Tone","hex":"#a06e57","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"earth tone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of a range of neutral colors reminiscent of natural materials, including soil, stone, and plants."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/earth_tone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Earth tone","blocks":[{"text":"Earth tone is a term used to describe a palette of colors that are similar to natural materials and landscapes. These colors are inspired by the earth's natural hues, including browns, greens, grays, and other warm and muted shades. Earth tones were popular in the 1970s and early 1980s during the environmental movement, as people sought to reconnect with nature and embrace more natural and organic lifestyles. In the mid-late 1980s, earth tones were supplanted by the neon pastels of Memphis Design."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth_tone"},{"title":"Earth","blocks":[{"text":"Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only astronomical object known to harbor life. This is made possible by Earth being an ocean world, the only one in the Solar System sustaining liquid surface water. Almost all of Earth's water is contained in its ocean, which covers 70.8% of Earth's crust. The remaining 29.2% of Earth's crust is dry land, which is predominantly located within Earth's land hemisphere in the form of continental landmasses. Most of Earth's land is at least somewhat humid and covered by vegetation, while large ice sheets at Earth's polar deserts retain more water than Earth's groundwater, lakes, rivers, and atmospheric water combined. Earth's crust consists of slowly moving tectonic plates, which interact to produce mountain ranges, volcanoes, and earthquakes. Earth has a liquid outer core that generates a magnetosphere capable of deflecting most of the destructive solar winds and cosmic radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg/330px-Meteosat-12-fci-march-equinox-2025-noon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth"},{"title":"Tint, shade and tone","blocks":[{"text":"In color theory, a tint is a mixture of a color with white, which increases lightness, while a shade is a mixture with black, which increases darkness. A tone is produced either by mixing a color with gray, or by both tinting and shading. Mixing a color with any neutral color reduces the chroma, or colorfulness, while the perceived hue can be affected slightly."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Tint-tone-shade.svg/330px-Tint-tone-shade.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tint%2C_shade_and_tone"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/500px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/1280px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg/500px-The_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A depiction of the early Solar System's protoplanetary disk from which Earth and other Solar System bodies were formed","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Mysterious_Case_of_the_Disappearing_Dust.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/NASA-EarlyEarth-PaleOrangeDot-20190802.jpg/500px-NASA-EarlyEarth-PaleOrangeDot-20190802.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/NASA-EarlyEarth-PaleOrangeDot-20190802.jpg/1280px-NASA-EarlyEarth-PaleOrangeDot-20190802.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/NASA-EarlyEarth-PaleOrangeDot-20190802.jpg/500px-NASA-EarlyEarth-PaleOrangeDot-20190802.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pale orange dot, an impression of Early Earth, featuring its tinted orange methane-rich early atmosphere[48]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANASA-EarlyEarth-PaleOrangeDot-20190802.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/500px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/1280px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/500px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An impression of the Archean, the eon after Earth's formation, featuring round stromatolites, which are early oxygen-producing forms of life from billions of years ago. After the Late Heavy Bombardment, Earth's crust had cooled; its water-rich barren surface is marked by continents and volcanoes, with the Moon still orbiting Earth half as far as it is today, appearing 2.8 times larger and producing strong tides.[61]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArchean.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Red_Giant_Earth_warm.jpg/500px-Red_Giant_Earth_warm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Red_Giant_Earth_warm.jpg/500px-Red_Giant_Earth_warm.jpg?utm_s